e is the bare `mean_absolute_deviation` function, adhering to its specific constraints (no external helpers, no validation, minimal comments).\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD) around the mean for a list of numbers.\n    It assumes valid, non-empty, finite numeric inputs and performs direct calculation.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    x_mean = sum(numbers) / n\n    sum_abs_deviations = sum(abs(x - x_mean) for x in numbers)\n    mad = sum_abs_deviations / n\n    return mad\n```"}
