{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef fruit_distribution(s,n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the count of mango fruits in a basket given a textual description of apples and oranges and the total fruit count.\n    Assumes valid input string and total number, and computes mangoes = total - apples - oranges.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Regex pattern to find a number followed by 'apple' or 'apples'\n    apple_pattern = r\"(\\d+)\\s*(?:apple|apples)\"\n    # Regex pattern to find a number followed by 'orange' or 'oranges'\n    orange_pattern = r\"(\\d+)\\s*(?:orange|oranges)\"\n\n    apples = 0\n    oranges = 0\n\n    # Find apples count\n    match_apples = re.search(apple_pattern, s)\n    if match_apples:\n        apples = int(match_apples.group(1))\n\n    # Find oranges count\n    match_oranges = re.search(orange_pattern, s)\n    if match_oranges:\n        oranges = int(match_oranges.group(1))\n        \n    # Calculate mangoes\n    mangoes = n - apples - oranges\n    \n    return mangoes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "import re\n\ndef fruit_distribution(s, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the number of mango fruits given a string of apples and oranges and total fruits.\n    Example: fruit_distribution(\"5 apples and 6 oranges\", 19) -> 8\n    \"\"\"\n    apples_count = 0\n    oranges_count = 0\n\n    # Regex to find numbers associated with \"apples\" or \"oranges\" (singular/plural, case-insensitive).\n    PARSE_REGEX = r\"(\\d+)\\s*(apples|apple|oranges|orange)\"\n\n    # Normalize the input string by making it lowercase for consistent matching.\n    normalized_s = s.lower()\n    \n    found_fruits = {}\n    matches = re.finditer(PARSE_REGEX, normalized_s)\n    \n    for match in matches:\n        count = int(match.group(1))\n        fruit_name = match.group(2)\n        if \"apple\" in fruit_name:\n            found_fruits[\"apples\"] = count\n        elif \"orange\" in fruit_name:\n            found_fruits[\"oranges\"] = count\n            \n    # Retrieve counts, defaulting to 0 if a fruit type isn't explicitly found.\n    apples_count = found_fruits.get(\"apples\", 0)\n    oranges_count = found_fruits.get(\"oranges\", 0)\n\n    # Compute mango count by subtracting apples and oranges from the total.\n    mango_count = n - (apples_count + oranges_count)\n    \n    return mango_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "def pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of non-negative integers, return the smallest even value and its first index.\n    If no even values or the array is empty, return [].\n    \"\"\"\n    smallest_even_value = None\n    smallest_even_index = None\n\n    for i, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            if smallest_even_value is None or value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = i\n    \n    if smallest_even_value is None:\n        return []\n    else:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "def pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even value and its first index in an array of non-negative integers.\n    Returns [value, index] or [] if no even value exists.\n    \"\"\"\n    smallest_even_value = None\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    for i, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            # If it's the first even value found, or it's smaller than the current smallest\n            if smallest_even_value is None or value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = i\n\n    if smallest_even_value is None:\n        return []\n    else:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "from collections import Counter\n\ndef search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the greatest positive integer x where its frequency in the list is >= x.\n    If no such integer exists, returns -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    freq_map = Counter(lst)\n    candidates = [val for val, count in freq_map.items() if val > 0 and count >= val]\n    return max(candidates) if candidates else -1"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "To generate the code based on the provided software design specification, I will create the directory structure and files as outlined in Section 3, then populate each file with the specified classes, methods, and logic. The critical requirements from the \"Original problem statement\" section will be applied to the `search` method's *internal implementation logic* within the `SearchService` class, meaning that method's core logic will be direct, avoid internal validation, and keep its docstring concise. The rest of the system will adhere to the detailed design.\n\nHere's the complete code for the project:\n\n```python\n# pyproject.toml\n# This file defines the project's build system and dependencies.\n# Install with `pip install .` or `poetry install` (if using poetry)\n\n[project]\nname = \"freqsearch\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"An application to find numbers with frequency >= value.\"\nauthors = [{ name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }]\nreadme = \"README.md\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = [\n    # No core dependencies beyond stdlib, but argparse for CLI.\n]\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\nhttp = [\"fastapi~=0.111.0\", \"uvicorn[standard]~=0.29.0\"]\ntest = [\"pytest~=8.2.0\", \"httpx~=0.27.0\", \"coverage~=7.5.0\"]\n\n[project.scripts]\nfreqsearch = \"freqsearch.main:cli_entrypoint\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n[tool.setuptools.package-dir]\nfreqsearch = \"src/freqsearch\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.console_scripts]\nfreqsearch = \"freqsearch.main:cli_entrypoint\"\n```\n\n```python\n# README.md\n# freqsearch\n#\n# This project implements a \"search\" algorithm that finds the greatest integer x > 0\n# such that its frequency in a given list is greater than or equal to x.\n# It exposes this functionality via a Python library, a CLI tool, and an optional HTTP JSON API.\n#\n# ## Installation\n#\n# ```bash\n# git clone <repository-url>\n# cd freqsearch\n# pip install .\n# # For HTTP API:\n# pip install \".[http]\"\n# ```\n#\n# ## Usage\n#\n# ### Python Library\n#\n# ```python\n# from freqsearch.services import SearchService\n# from freqsearch.repositories import FrequencyRepository\n# from freqsearch.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n#\n# service = SearchService(FrequencyRepository())\n#\n# try:\n#     result = service.search([4, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1])\n#     print(f\"Result: {result}\") # Expected: 2\n#\n#     result = service.search([5, 5, 4, 4, 4])\n#     print(f\"Result: {result}\") # Expected: -1\n#\n#     service.search([]) # This will raise InvalidInputError\n# except InvalidInputError as e:\n#     print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n# ```\n#\n# ### CLI Tool\n#\n# ```bash\n# freqsearch search --list \"4,1,2,2,3,1\"\n# # Output: 2\n#\n# freqsearch search --json \"[5,5,4,4,4]\"\n# # Output: -1\n#\n# freqsearch search --list \"invalid,input\"\n# # Output: Error: Invalid input: Cannot parse 'invalid' as integer.\n# # Exit code: 2\n# ```\n#\n# ### HTTP API (Optional)\n#\n# First, start the server:\n# ```bash\n# freqsearch server --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000\n# ```\n#\n# Then, make a request using `curl` or similar:\n# ```bash\n# curl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"numbers\": [4,1,2,2,3,1]}' http://127.0.0.1:8000/search\n# # Response: {\"result\": 2}\n#\n# curl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"numbers\": [5,5,4,4,4]}' http://127.0.0.1:8000/search\n# # Response: {\"result\": -1}\n#\n# curl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"numbers\": [1,\"a\",2]}' http://127.0.0.1:8000/search\n# # Response: {\"error\":\"Invalid input: All elements must be integers.\"} (HTTP 400)\n# ```\n#\n# ## Development\n#\n# Running tests:\n# ```bash\n# pip install \".[test]\"\n# pytest\n# ```\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/__init__.py\n\"\"\"Package metadata for freqsearch.\"\"\"\n\n__version__ = \"0.1.0\"\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/exceptions.py\n\"\"\"Domain-specific exceptions.\"\"\"\n\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input data.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/models.py\n\"\"\"Data Transfer Objects (DTOs) for internal communication and APIs.\"\"\"\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass SearchRequest:\n    \"\"\"Represents a request to search for numbers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, numbers: List[int]):\n        self.numbers = numbers\n\nclass SearchResult:\n    \"\"\"Represents the result of a search operation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, result: int):\n        self.result = result\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/utils.py\n\"\"\"Utility functions for parsing and validating input.\"\"\"\nfrom typing import List, Any\nimport json\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\ndef parse_comma_separated(s: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Parses a comma-separated string of integers into a list.\"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n    parts = [p.strip() for p in s.split(\",\")]\n    numbers = []\n    for p in parts:\n        if p == \"\":\n            continue\n        try:\n            numbers.append(int(p))\n        except ValueError:\n            raise InvalidInputError(f\"Cannot parse '{p}' as integer.\")\n    return numbers\n\ndef validate_numbers(numbers: List[Any]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates that the input is a non-empty list of integers.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if not numbers:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n    for num in numbers:\n        if not isinstance(num, int):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"All elements must be integers.\")\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/repositories.py\n\"\"\"Abstraction for frequency counting.\"\"\"\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom typing import List, Dict\n\nclass FrequencyRepository:\n    \"\"\"Handles counting frequencies of numbers.\"\"\"\n    def count(self, numbers: List[int]) -> Dict[int, int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns a dictionary mapping each number to its frequency.\"\"\"\n        # Validation is typically handled upstream by controllers.\n        return dict(Counter(numbers))\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/services.py\n\"\"\"Core algorithm and business logic.\"\"\"\nfrom typing import List, Dict\nfrom .repositories import FrequencyRepository\n\nclass SearchService:\n    \"\"\"Implements the search algorithm to find the greatest integer.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, freq_repo: FrequencyRepository):\n        self._freq_repo = freq_repo\n\n    def search(self, numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Finds the greatest integer x > 0 such that its frequency is >= x.\n        Returns -1 if no such integer exists.\n        \"\"\"\n        freqs: Dict[int, int] = self._freq_repo.count(numbers)\n        candidates = [v for v, c in freqs.items() if v > 0 and c >= v]\n        return max(candidates) if candidates else -1\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/controllers.py\n\"\"\"Orchestrates input validation and service invocation.\"\"\"\nfrom .models import SearchRequest, SearchResult\nfrom .services import SearchService\nfrom .utils import validate_numbers\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass SearchController:\n    \"\"\"Handles search requests, validates input, and delegates to the service.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, service: SearchService):\n        self._service = service\n\n    def handle_search_request(self, req: SearchRequest) -> SearchResult:\n        \"\"\"Processes a search request and returns the result.\"\"\"\n        validate_numbers(req.numbers) # Validate numbers from the request model\n        result = self._service.search(req.numbers)\n        return SearchResult(result=result)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/adapters.py\n\"\"\"CLI and HTTP adapters for external interfaces.\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport sys\nimport json\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\nfrom .controllers import SearchController\nfrom .models import SearchRequest, SearchResult\nfrom .utils import parse_comma_separated, validate_numbers\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n# Conditional import for FastAPI (optional HTTP adapter)\ntry:\n    from fastapi import FastAPI, Request, HTTPException\n    from pydantic import BaseModel\n    import uvicorn\n    HTTP_ADAPTER_AVAILABLE = True\nexcept ImportError:\n    HTTP_ADAPTER_AVAILABLE = False\n    class FastAPI: # Mock FastAPI for type hinting if not installed\n        pass\n    class BaseModel:\n        pass\n    def uvicorn_run(*args, **kwargs):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"Uvicorn not installed. Install with 'pip install freqsearch[http]'\")\n    uvicorn = type('uvicorn_mock', (object,), {'run': uvicorn_run})()\n\n\nclass CLIAdapter:\n    \"\"\"Adapter for command-line interface interactions.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, controller: SearchController):\n        self._controller = controller\n\n    def parse_args(self, argv: List[str]) -> SearchRequest:\n        \"\"\"Parses command-line arguments into a SearchRequest.\"\"\"\n        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n            prog=\"freqsearch search\",\n            description=\"Finds the greatest integer x > 0 where frequency(x) >= x.\"\n        )\n        group = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)\n        group.add_argument(\n            \"--list\",\n            type=str,\n            help=\"Comma-separated list of integers (e.g., '1,2,2,3').\"\n        )\n        group.add_argument(\n            \"--json\",\n            type=str,\n            help=\"JSON array of integers (e.g., '[1,2,2,3]').\"\n        )\n\n        args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n        numbers: List[int]\n\n        if args.list:\n            numbers = parse_comma_separated(args.list)\n        elif args.json:\n            try:\n                parsed_json = json.loads(args.json)\n                numbers = parsed_json # Validation will check if it's a list of ints\n            except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n                raise InvalidInputError(f\"Invalid JSON format: {e}\")\n        else:\n            # Should not happen due to required=True and mutually_exclusive_group\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"No input provided. Use --list or --json.\")\n        \n        return SearchRequest(numbers=numbers)\n\n    def run(self, argv: List[str]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Executes the CLI command and prints the result or error.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            req = self.parse_args(argv)\n            res = self._controller.handle_search_request(req)\n            print(res.result)\n            return 0\n        except InvalidInputError as e:\n            print(f\"Error: Invalid input: {e.message}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"Error: An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 1\n\n\nif HTTP_ADAPTER_AVAILABLE:\n    class HTTPRequestModel(BaseModel):\n        numbers: List[int]\n\n    class HTTPResponseModel(BaseModel):\n        result: int\n\n    class HTTPErrorModel(BaseModel):\n        error: str\n\n    class HTTPAdapter:\n        \"\"\"Adapter for HTTP JSON API using FastAPI.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, controller: SearchController):\n            self._controller = controller\n            self.app = FastAPI(\n                title=\"FreqSearch API\",\n                description=\"API for the greatest integer search algorithm.\",\n                version=\"0.1.0\"\n            )\n            self._setup_routes()\n\n        def _setup_routes(self):\n            @self.app.post(\"/search\", response_model=HTTPResponseModel, responses={400: {\"model\": HTTPErrorModel}})\n            async def search_endpoint(request_data: HTTPRequestModel):\n                try:\n                    search_req = SearchRequest(numbers=request_data.numbers)\n                    search_res = self._controller.handle_search_request(search_req)\n                    return HTTPResponseModel(result=search_res.result)\n                except InvalidInputError as e:\n                    raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail={\"error\": f\"Invalid input: {e.message}\"})\n                except Exception as e:\n                    raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail={\"error\": f\"Internal server error: {e}\"})\n\n        def start(self, host: str, port: int) -> None:\n            \"\"\"Starts the FastAPI server.\"\"\"\n            uvicorn.run(self.app, host=host, port=port)\nelse:\n    class HTTPAdapter:\n        \"\"\"Mock HTTPAdapter if FastAPI is not installed.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, controller: SearchController):\n            self._controller = controller\n            self.app = None # No FastAPI app\n            print(\"Warning: FastAPI not installed. HTTP API will not be available.\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n        def start(self, host: str, port: int) -> None:\n            \"\"\"Raises an error if trying to start HTTP server without FastAPI.\"\"\"\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                \"HTTP server dependencies (fastapi, uvicorn) are not installed. \"\n                \"Install with 'pip install freqsearch[http]'.\"\n            )\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/main.py\n\"\"\"Main entry points for CLI and HTTP server.\"\"\"\nimport sys\nimport argparse\nfrom typing import List\n\nfrom .adapters import CLIAdapter, HTTPAdapter, HTTP_ADAPTER_AVAILABLE\nfrom .controllers import SearchController\nfrom .services import SearchService\nfrom .repositories import FrequencyRepository\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nDEFAULT_HOST = \"127.0.0.1\"\nDEFAULT_PORT = 8000\n\ndef _get_search_controller() -> SearchController:\n    \"\"\"Helper to instantiate the dependency graph for search.\"\"\"\n    freq_repo = FrequencyRepository()\n    service = SearchService(freq_repo)\n    controller = SearchController(service)\n    return controller\n\ndef run_cli(argv: List[str]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Runs the CLI entry point for the 'search' command.\"\"\"\n    # The `cli_entrypoint` below handles dispatching 'search' or 'server'.\n    # This `run_cli` is specifically for the 'search' subcommand.\n    controller = _get_search_controller()\n    adapter = CLIAdapter(controller)\n    return adapter.run(argv)\n\ndef run_server(host: str = DEFAULT_HOST, port: int = DEFAULT_PORT) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Starts the HTTP server.\"\"\"\n    if not HTTP_ADAPTER_AVAILABLE:\n        print(\n            \"Error: HTTP server dependencies (fastapi, uvicorn) are not installed. \"\n            \"Install with 'pip install freqsearch[http]'.\",\n            file=sys.stderr\n        )\n        sys.exit(1)\n    \n    controller = _get_search_controller()\n    adapter = HTTPAdapter(controller)\n    print(f\"Starting HTTP server on http://{host}:{port}\")\n    try:\n        adapter.start(host=host, port=port)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error starting HTTP server: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        sys.exit(1)\n\ndef cli_entrypoint():\n    \"\"\"Main entry point for the `freqsearch` console script.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        prog=\"freqsearch\",\n        description=\"A tool for searching numbers based on frequency criteria, with CLI and HTTP interfaces.\"\n    )\n    \n    subparsers = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True, help=\"Available commands\")\n\n    # Search subcommand\n    search_parser = subparsers.add_parser(\"search\", help=\"Run the frequency search algorithm.\")\n    search_parser.add_argument(\n        \"--list\", type=str, help=\"Comma-separated list of integers (e.g., '1,2,2,3').\"\n    )\n    search_parser.add_argument(\n        \"--json\", type=str, help=\"JSON array of integers (e.g., '[1,2,2,3]').\"\n    )\n\n    # Server subcommand\n    server_parser = subparsers.add_parser(\"server\", help=\"Start the optional HTTP API server.\")\n    server_parser.add_argument(\n        \"--host\", type=str, default=DEFAULT_HOST, help=f\"Host address for the server (default: {DEFAULT_HOST}).\"\n    )\n    server_parser.add_argument(\n        \"--port\", type=int, default=DEFAULT_PORT, help=f\"Port for the server (default: {DEFAULT_PORT}).\"\n    )\n\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    if args.command == \"search\":\n        # Pass the arguments specific to the 'search' command to run_cli\n        # This requires reconstructing argv to mimic the CLIAdapter's expected input\n        search_argv = []\n        if args.list:\n            search_argv.extend([\"--list\", args.list])\n        if args.json:\n            search_argv.extend([\"--json\", args.json])\n        sys.exit(run_cli(search_argv))\n    elif args.command == \"server\":\n        run_server(host=args.host, port=args.port)\n    else:\n        parser.print_help()\n        sys.exit(1)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/freqsearch/__main__.py\n\"\"\"Console entry point for `python -m freqsearch`.\"\"\"\nfrom .main import cli_entrypoint\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    cli_entrypoint()\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_services.py\n# FORBIDDEN: DO NOT write unit tests - This file is a placeholder.\n# Its contents would normally include tests for SearchService.\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_controllers.py\n# FORBIDDEN: DO NOT write unit tests - This file is a placeholder.\n# Its contents would normally include tests for SearchController.\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_cli.py\n# FORBIDDEN: DO NOT write unit tests - This file is a placeholder.\n# Its contents would normally include tests for CLIAdapter.\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "def strange_sort_list(lst):\n    '''\n    Given list of integers, return list in strange order.\n    Alternates picking minimum and maximum remaining elements.\n    '''\n    n = len(lst)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return list(lst)\n\n    # Create a sorted copy of the input list\n    arr = sorted(lst)\n\n    l, r = 0, n - 1\n    out = []\n    toggle = True # True for min (left pointer), False for max (right pointer)\n\n    while l <= r:\n        if toggle:\n            out.append(arr[l])\n            l += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(arr[r])\n            r -= 1\n        toggle = not toggle\n        \n    return out"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "def strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers in a strange order: min, max, min, max...\n    Handles empty lists and preserves duplicates as per requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    sorted_vals = sorted(lst)\n    \n    left = 0\n    right = len(sorted_vals) - 1\n    result = []\n    take_min = True\n\n    while left <= right:\n        if take_min:\n            result.append(sorted_vals[left])\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            result.append(sorted_vals[right])\n            right -= 1\n        take_min = not take_min\n        \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1.\n    '''\n    # Sides must be positive real numbers.\n    if a <= 0 or b <= 0 or c <= 0:\n        return -1\n\n    # Triangle validity uses strict inequality: sum of any two sides strictly greater than third.\n    if not (a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a):\n        return -1\n\n    # Compute semi-perimeter\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2.0\n\n    # Compute area using Heron's formula\n    val_under_sqrt = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n\n    # Clamp very small negative values due to floating point inaccuracies to zero.\n    if val_under_sqrt < 0 and abs(val_under_sqrt) < 1e-12:\n        val_under_sqrt = 0.0\n    \n    # If val_under_sqrt is still negative, it indicates an unexpected numerical error\n    # or a logical flaw not covered by strict inequality, return -1 as it's invalid.\n    if val_under_sqrt < 0:\n        return -1\n\n    area = math.sqrt(val_under_sqrt)\n\n    # Round the area to 2 decimal places using Python's built-in round (half-to-even).\n    return round(area, 2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "import math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle, return its area rounded to 2 decimal points.\n    Returns -1 if the sides do not form a valid triangle (sides positive, triangle inequality).\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate that all side lengths are positive numbers.\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0):\n        return -1\n\n    # Check the triangle inequality: the sum of any two sides must be strictly greater than the third.\n    if not (a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a):\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate the semi-perimeter.\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2.0\n\n    # Calculate the area using Heron's formula. Handle potential floating point inaccuracies.\n    under_sqrt = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n    if under_sqrt < -1e-9:  # If significantly negative due to errors, it's an invalid case.\n        return -1\n    \n    # Clamp to zero if very slightly negative (due to float precision for degenerate triangles).\n    area = math.sqrt(max(0.0, under_sqrt))\n\n    # Round the area to 2 decimal places.\n    return round(area, 2)"}
