{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "```python\ndef is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given integer is the product of exactly three prime numbers.\n    The function considers prime factors with multiplicity.\"\"\"\n\n    def get_prime_factors_inline(n):\n        \"\"\"Helper function to find prime factors of n, including multiplicity.\"\"\"\n        factors = []\n        if n < 2:\n            return factors\n        \n        d = 2\n        temp_n = n\n        while d * d <= temp_n:\n            while temp_n % d == 0:\n                factors.append(d)\n                temp_n //= d\n            d += 1 if d == 2 else 2 # Increment by 1 for d=2, then by 2 for odd numbers\n        \n        if temp_n > 1: # If temp_n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself.\n            factors.append(temp_n)\n        return factors\n\n    factors = get_prime_factors_inline(a)\n    return len(factors) == 3\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "```python\ndef is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns True if x is n**k for some integer k >= 0.\n    Assumes x and n are positive integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return x == 1\n\n    # For n >= 2 and x > 1, repeatedly divide x by n.\n    current_x = x\n    while current_x > 1 and current_x % n == 0:\n        current_x //= n\n\n    return current_x == 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "def is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is the product of exactly three prime numbers.\n    Assumes 'a' is an integer less than 100 and valid.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _get_prime_factors_list(n_val):\n        factors = []\n        d = 2\n        temp = n_val\n        while d * d <= temp:\n            while temp % d == 0:\n                factors.append(d)\n                temp //= d\n            d += 1\n        if temp > 1:\n            factors.append(temp)\n        return factors\n\n    prime_factors = _get_prime_factors_list(a)\n    return len(prime_factors) == 3"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "def is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if x is a simple integer power of n (n**k == x for k >= 0).\n    Assumes n >= 1 and x >= 0 as per design specification preconditions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return False\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n    if n == 1:\n        return False\n\n    current_x = x\n    while current_x % n == 0:\n        current_x //= n\n    \n    return current_x == 1"}
