{"task_id": "HumanEval/77", "solution": "def iscube(a):\n    '''\n    Checks if an integer is a perfect cube using an integer-only binary search.\n    Assumes the input 'a' is a valid integer.\n    '''\n    if a == 0:\n        return True\n\n    is_negative = a < 0\n    abs_a = abs(a)\n\n    # Perform binary search to find the integer cube root (floor) of abs_a.\n    # The search range is set from 0 up to abs_a for correctness with arbitrary-precision integers.\n    low, high = 0, abs_a\n    root_candidate = 0\n\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        mid_cubed = pow(mid, 3)  # Use built-in pow for integer exponentiation\n\n        if mid_cubed == abs_a:\n            root_candidate = mid  # Found the exact integer cube root\n            break\n        elif mid_cubed < abs_a:\n            root_candidate = mid  # This 'mid' is a potential floor, try a larger value\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:  # mid_cubed > abs_a\n            high = mid - 1  # This 'mid' is too large, try a smaller value\n    \n    # After the loop, root_candidate holds the largest integer 'r' such that r^3 <= abs_a.\n    # Reapply the original sign to the found root candidate.\n    final_root = -root_candidate if is_negative else root_candidate\n    \n    # Check if the cube of this final_root exactly equals the original input 'a'.\n    return pow(final_root, 3) == a"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/77", "solution": "```python\ndef iscube(a):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if an integer is a perfect cube.\n    Handles zero, positive, and negative integers efficiently using binary search.\n    \"\"\"\n    if a == 0:\n        return True\n\n    original_a = a\n    is_negative = False\n    if a < 0:\n        is_negative = True\n        a = -a  # Work with absolute value for binary search\n\n    low = 0\n    # Determine a suitable upper bound for the cube root using bit_length\n    # to ensure O(log N) complexity relative to the number of bits.\n    high = 1 << ((a.bit_length() + 2) // 3)\n    high = max(high, 2) # Ensures upper bound is at least 2 for a >= 1.\n\n    root_abs = 0  # This will hold floor(abs(a)**(1/3))\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        mid_cubed = mid * mid * mid\n\n        if mid_cubed == a:\n            root_abs = mid\n            break\n        elif mid_cubed < a:\n            low = mid + 1\n            root_abs = mid\n        else:  # mid_cubed > a\n            high = mid - 1\n            \n    # After the binary search, root_abs holds floor(abs(original_a)**(1/3)).\n    # Check if the cube of this root actually equals the absolute value of the input.\n    if root_abs * root_abs * root_abs != a:\n        return False\n    \n    # If it is a perfect cube in absolute terms, verify with the original sign.\n    if is_negative:\n        return (-root_abs) * (-root_abs) * (-root_abs) == original_a\n    else:\n        return root_abs * root_abs * root_abs == original_a\n\n```"}
