{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string of square brackets contains a valid nested subsequence like '[[]]'.\n    It uses a single pass to track open brackets and detect the required pattern.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    open_bracket_indices = []\n    # This flag becomes True when an inner '[]' has been matched, and an outer '[' still exists.\n    potential_nested_found = False \n\n    for char_index, char in enumerate(string):\n        if char == '[':\n            open_bracket_indices.append(char_index)\n        elif char == ']':\n            if not open_bracket_indices:\n                # No open bracket to match, so this ']' is skipped.\n                continue\n            \n            # This ']' matches the most recently opened '['.\n            # We pop it as it forms a pair with the current closing bracket.\n            _ = open_bracket_indices.pop() \n\n            if potential_nested_found:\n                # If we've already identified a `[[ ]` state and now find another `]`,\n                # this completes the `[[ ] ]` nested subsequence.\n                return True\n            \n            # If after popping an inner bracket, there's still an open bracket on the stack,\n            # it means we have at least a `[[ ` pattern followed by a `]`. This forms `[[ ]`.\n            if open_bracket_indices:\n                potential_nested_found = True\n            else:\n                # The stack became empty, meaning the current `[]` was a simple, non-nested pair.\n                # The `potential_nested_found` flag is reset if it was set, as there's no outer `[` left.\n                potential_nested_found = False\n    \n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "def check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(txt):\n    '''\n    Returns True if the last character of a string is an alphabetical character\n    and is not part of a word, False otherwise.\n    '''\n    tokens = txt.split(' ')\n\n    last_token = tokens[-1]\n\n    if last_token == \"\":\n        return False\n\n    if len(last_token) != 1:\n        return False\n\n    char = last_token[0]\n    return 'a' <= char <= 'z' or 'A' <= char <= 'Z'"}
