{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "ROMAN_MAP = [\n    (1000, \"M\"), (900, \"CM\"), (500, \"D\"), (400, \"CD\"),\n    (100, \"C\"), (90, \"XC\"), (50, \"L\"), (40, \"XL\"),\n    (10, \"X\"), (9, \"IX\"), (5, \"V\"), (4, \"IV\"), (1, \"I\")\n]\n\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a positive integer (1-1000) to its Roman numeral equivalent in lowercase.\n    Assumes input 'number' is an integer within the valid range.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    n = number\n    for value, numeral in ROMAN_MAP:\n        while n >= value:\n            result.append(numeral)\n            n -= value\n    return ''.join(result).lower()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "def int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer, obtain its roman numeral equivalent as a string,\n    and return it in lowercase. Restrictions: 1 <= num <= 1000.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Define the Roman numeral mapping from largest to smallest values.\n    # This list is used for the greedy conversion algorithm.\n    roman_map = [\n        (1000, \"M\"), (900, \"CM\"), (500, \"D\"), (400, \"CD\"),\n        (100, \"C\"), (90, \"XC\"), (50, \"L\"), (40, \"XL\"),\n        (10, \"X\"), (9, \"IX\"), (5, \"V\"), (4, \"IV\"), (1, \"I\")\n    ]\n\n    result = []\n    n = number\n    for value, symbol in roman_map:\n        while n >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            n -= value\n    return \"\".join(result).lower()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "```python\ndef even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer, return a tuple with the number of even and odd digits respectively.\n    Leading signs are ignored, and '0' is counted as an even digit.\n    \"\"\"\n    even_count = 0\n    odd_count = 0\n\n    abs_num = abs(num)\n\n    # Handle the special case where the number itself is 0.\n    if abs_num == 0:\n        return (1, 0)\n\n    # Iterate through the digits of the absolute number.\n    current_num = abs_num\n    while current_num > 0:\n        digit = current_num % 10\n        if digit % 2 == 0:\n            even_count += 1\n        else:\n            odd_count += 1\n        current_num //= 10\n\n    return (even_count, odd_count)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "def right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given three side lengths, determine if they form a right-angled triangle.\n    This implementation uses a small tolerance for floating-point comparison.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Sort the sides to identify the potential hypotenuse (largest side)\n    sides = sorted([a, b, c])\n    s1, s2, s3 = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n\n    # Calculate squares of the sides\n    s1_sq = s1 * s1\n    s2_sq = s2 * s2\n    s3_sq = s3 * s3\n\n    # Use a small tolerance for floating-point comparisons to account for precision issues\n    tolerance = 1e-9\n\n    # Check if the sum of squares of the two shorter sides equals the square of the longest side\n    return abs(s1_sq + s2_sq - s3_sq) <= tolerance"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Returns the word with the maximum unique characters from a list of strings.\n    If there's a tie, the lexicographically smallest word is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    max_unique_count = -1\n    result_word = \"\"\n\n    for word in words:\n        current_unique_count = len(set(word))\n\n        if current_unique_count > max_unique_count:\n            max_unique_count = current_unique_count\n            result_word = word\n        elif current_unique_count == max_unique_count:\n            # Apply lexicographical tie-breaker\n            if word < result_word:\n                result_word = word\n    \n    return result_word"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Returns the word from a list with the maximum number of unique characters.\n    Ties are broken by lexicographical order.\"\"\"\n\n    word_metrics = []\n    for word in words:\n        unique_count = len(set(word))\n        word_metrics.append((unique_count, word))\n\n    max_unique_count = 0\n    if word_metrics:\n        max_unique_count = max(metric[0] for metric in word_metrics)\n\n    best_words = []\n    for unique_count, word in word_metrics:\n        if unique_count == max_unique_count:\n            best_words.append(word)\n\n    return min(best_words)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    '''\n    sides = sorted([a, b, c])\n    x, y, z = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n    epsilon = 1e-9\n    return abs((x * x) + (y * y) - (z * z)) <= epsilon\n```"}
